@page "/pdf"

<CatHead Title="PDF documents">
    A PDF in the page, drawn by the browser's own viewer, with a bar you write and a way out where the
    browser has no viewer. Nothing is loaded for it: no script and no PDF engine.
</CatHead>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Pdf.Frame" Title="Frame with a bar" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <code>.pdf-frame</code> holds a <code>.pdf-frame-bar</code> — the file's
        <code>.pdf-frame-name</code> and your actions, open in a new tab and download — and an
        <code>&lt;object type="application/pdf"&gt;</code>, which takes the rest of
        <code>--pdf-height</code>. <strong>Write a fallback inside the object.</strong> Where the browser
        can draw the PDF it does; where it cannot — its viewer switched off, a headless browser, a phone
        that only offers the file — it shows what you wrote inside instead, centred in the frame. Give the
        object an <code>aria-label</code> naming the document.
    </p>
    <p>
        Chrome, Edge and Safari on a computer draw every page with their own toolbar. Safari on iPhone
        and iPad draws the document without a toolbar and may show only its first page, so keep
        <em>Open</em> in the bar: it hands the file to the system's own reader. Write the fallback every
        time, and never decide it from <code>navigator.pdfViewerEnabled</code>: a browser can report a
        viewer and still draw the fallback, as the WebKit build this catalogue is tested in does, while
        headless Chromium reports none.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Pdf.Page" Title="One page at a time" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <code>.pdf-frame--page</code> gives the frame the shape of a page instead of a height —
        <code>--media-ratio</code>, as every framed medium reads it, A4 unless you set another — for a document read a page at a time beside
        something else, a form or a list. <code>#view=FitH</code> on the address asks a viewer that
        understands it to fit the page's width.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Pdf.InACard" Title="Inside a card" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        In a card the head names the document and carries its actions, so the frame needs no bar.
        <code>.pdf-frame--flush</code> gives up the frame's own border and corners, so the card's are
        the only ones and the document meets its edges. Keep the fallback inside the object.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatSnippet Name="Pdf/Iframe.html" Title="Iframe on managed machines">
    <p>
        An <code>&lt;iframe&gt;</code> works in the same frame, but it has no fallback: where there is no
        viewer it downloads the file or stays empty. Use it only where you know every reader's browser —
        an internal tool on managed machines — and keep the download in the bar.
    </p>
</CatSnippet>
